          "citation": "100 Haw. 335, 60 P.3d 285 (2002)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Hawai'i",
          "year": 2002,
          "date_decided": "2002-12-26",
          "holding": "The court held that incarceration does not itself forfeit parental rights but may be weighed in the safe-family-home analysis, and separately that alleged ADA violations are not a defense to a termination proceeding; it affirmed termination after finding DHS made reasonable efforts.",
          "relevance": "It frames how a service plan must be assessed for an incarcerated or otherwise disabled parent rather than treating status as dispositive.",
          "quote": "a criminal charge, conviction, or incarceration does not per se result in the forfeiture of parental rights, but confinement can be considered a factor",
